==============================================================================
STORAGE OVERHEAD BY PAYLOAD SIZE (100 records each)
==============================================================================
  Payload            |  Payload B |  Compressed B |       File B |   Per-record B |   Fixed B
  ------------------ | ---------- | ------------- | ------------ | -------------- | ---------
  tiny (~10 B)       |         10 |            12 |       24,841 |          248.4 |     236.4
  small (~100 B)     |        100 |            13 |       24,841 |          248.4 |     235.4
  medium (~1 KB)     |       1024 |            18 |       25,909 |          259.1 |     241.1
  large (~10 KB)     |      10240 |            34 |       28,573 |          285.7 |     251.7

  Interpretation:
    - 'Compressed B' is the zlib-compressed payload.
    - 'Fixed B' is per-record bytes that are not payload:
        key_id (2) + version (8) + timestamp (8) + nonce (16)
        + HMAC (16) + key digest (32) + base64 expansion.
    - Repeated 'x' characters compress extremely well; real
      data will show a larger 'Compressed B' and lower 'Fixed B'.

==============================================================================
WHOLE-FILE REPLAY — honest demonstration of the documented gap
==============================================================================
  🔴 Attacker replaced file with authentic older snapshot
  Current value (before attack): 9999
  Loaded value (after attack):   1000
  Exception raised by system:    False
  Silent stale replay:           YES (gap confirmed)

  Result: whole-file replay is NOT detectable by container MAC alone.
  The file is genuinely authentic. Detection requires an external
  witness — a monotonic counter stored outside the file.

  Mitigations:
    - Short TTL (records expire before replay becomes useful)
    - External counter (write count to a log server or blockchain)
    - Cross-file chain (each save() commits to the previous save hash)

